# Color Picker

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A colour picker lets a person choose a colour, such as a label colour or the colour of a light. Home Assistant has more than one way to do this, and they differ in how well they work without sight or a mouse. This page says which to offer.

This page covers:

- Color Picker
  - `<input type="color">`
  - `ha-color-picker`
  - `ha-hs-color-picker`
  - `ha-rgb-color-picker`
  - `ha-color-temp-picker`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

1. A label that says what the colour is for.
2. The control: a list of named colours, a slider, a colour wheel or a hex box.
3. A written value for the choice, such as “Amber” or “Warm white, 2,700 K”.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-input-type-color"><code>&lt;input type="color"&gt;</code></h3>

The browser’s own colour input. The value is a hex code such as `#ffbf47`, and it starts at `#000000`. It opens the device’s colour dialog, so its look and keyboard behaviour depend on the device. It needs a visible label. Offer a hex text box or named colours beside it.

Home Assistant uses it inside the `color_rgb` selector, which stores red, green and blue values.

<h3 id="child-ha-color-picker"><code>ha-color-picker</code></h3>

A searchable list of named colours. Each row has a colour circle and a name, so colour is never the only cue. It offers primary, accent, red, pink, purple, blue, teal, green, amber, orange, grey, black, white and others, and a typed search can add a custom colour. It can also offer “None” and “State”.

In YAML this is the `ui_color` selector:

```yaml
ui_color:
  include_none: true
  include_state: false
  default_color: primary
```

Home Assistant uses it for label colours and icon colours. Named colours follow the theme’s colour variables, so describe the colour by its name, not by a hex value.

<h3 id="child-ha-hs-color-picker"><code>ha-hs-color-picker</code></h3>

A round hue and saturation wheel, drawn on a canvas. It is used in the light dialog. It is operated by dragging. It has no keyboard control and no name or role for a screen reader, so it cannot be used without a mouse or touch. It has no style keys.

Never offer it as the only way. Offer named colours, a colour temperature slider, a brightness slider or a hex box as well.

<h3 id="child-ha-rgb-color-picker"><code>ha-rgb-color-picker</code></h3>

This Home Assistant version does not use the name `ha-rgb-color-picker`. RGB colour is chosen in two places instead. In the light dialog, the wheel above sits with sliders for white, cold white and warm white. In a form, the `color_rgb` selector shows the browser’s colour input.

<h3 id="child-ha-color-temp-picker"><code>ha-color-temp-picker</code></h3>

This Home Assistant version does not use the name `ha-color-temp-picker`. Colour temperature is a slider from warm to cool, in three places:

- The light dialog has a slider.
- The `color_temp` selector shows a labelled slider with a warm-to-cool gradient. It takes `unit` (`kelvin` or `mired`, mired by default), `min` and `max`. The default range is 153 to 500 mired.
- The `light-color-temp` card feature adds the slider to a tile card.

The gradient is drawn by Home Assistant. The theme cannot change it, and it does not promise to match a real lamp. Write the value in words, such as “Warm white, 2,700 K”.

What Civica sets for these controls is general: the text colour, the outline colour and the focus ring (3px wide, offset 2px). It sets no key for pickers.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- Named colours are the most accessible choice and need no sight of the exact shade.
- The browser colour input depends on the device, and its dialog is not under your control.
- The wheel fails keyboard and screen-reader use in this version.
- There are no verified card-mod selectors for any of these.
- The pickers appear in Home Assistant’s editors and dialogs. A dashboard author places only the `light-color-temp` card feature.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Name every colour in words. Show a preview with text, such as “Warm white, 2,700 K”. Do not require anyone to tell colours apart. Keep palettes short. Do not rely on dragging; a slider or a typed value is the alternative.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: every choice has a name.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard, and 2.5.7 Dragging Movements: the wheel fails these without an alternative.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: the wheel has no name or role.
- 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions and 4.1.3 Status Messages.

For the colour and light controls on a dashboard, see [Lighting capabilities and colour](../home-assistant/lighting.html) and [Helpers and scalar input controls](../home-assistant/helpers-and-scalar-inputs.html). Use [Sliders and precise values](sliders.html) for the slider. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

